<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Chiefdom of Kauai</h1><p class="st-meta">1400 CE – 1824 CE (approx.) · capital Waimea</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of Kauaʻi and Niʻihau, the only Hawaiian realm never conquered by Kamehameha I, which joined the Kingdom of Hawaii by agreement in 1810.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kauai">Kauaʻi</a> was settled early in Hawaiian history, and by around 1400 its districts were united under a single aliʻi nui, the paramount chief or mōʻī, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waimea-kauai">Waimea</a> on the south-west coast. Island genealogies trace a long line of rulers, though dates before the 18th century rest on oral tradition.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Chiefs governed through the kapu system of sacred restrictions and a hierarchy of district and land-section chiefs; commoners farmed irrigated taro and tended fishponds. The wide <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaieiewaho-channel">Kaʻieʻiewaho Channel</a> protected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kauai">Kauaʻi</a> from invasion. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waimea-kauai">Waimea</a> in January 1778, the first European contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaeokulani">Kaʻeokulani</a>, who ruled Kauaʻi and for a time <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a>, died in 1794, leaving the island to his young son Kaʻumualiʻi.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a> twice prepared invasions, in 1796 and 1804, but storms and epidemic stopped him. In 1810 Kaʻumualiʻi agreed to become his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. After a Russian adventure in 1816 to 1817, Kaʻumualiʻi was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a> in 1821 and died there in 1824; his son's revolt that year failed.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1400 CE – 1824 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry